invariant: INV-17
file: aes_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh aes-equiv
reason: the x86-64 AES-128 key expansion keeps its round key in a volatile
  variable, so each round key passes through a stack slot and the last,
  from which the expansion runs backwards to the key, stays in the dead
  frame after the call. Under a SUITE=aesgcm build that key is a traffic
  key. Every schedule is still right, so no vector notices.
  bin/aes_equiv_test clears the stack below a call, makes one expansion
  and looks for any word of the schedule in the bytes the call wrote.
  Both architectures read the line, and the script builds that binary
  for each and runs it under qemu.

--- old
    aes_state round_key = load_block(key);
--- new
    volatile aes_state round_key = load_block(key);
